# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_OBJECT_GET_INFO */
int64_t object_get_info(uint32_t handle, uint32_t topic, void *buffer,
                        uint64_t buffer_size, uint64_t *actual,
                        uint64_t *avail);
/* CROI_SYS_OBJECT_GET_PROPERTY */
int64_t object_get_property(uint32_t handle, uint32_t property, void *value,
                            uint64_t size);
/* CROI_SYS_OBJECT_SET_PROPERTY */
int64_t object_set_property(uint32_t handle, uint32_t property,
                            const void *value, uint64_t size);
/* CROI_SYS_OBJECT_GET_CHILD */
int64_t object_get_child(uint32_t parent, uint64_t koid, uint32_t rights,
                         uint32_t *out);

int64_t croi_vmo_size(uint32_t vmo, uint64_t *size);
```

# DESCRIPTION

## object_get_info

`object_get_info` copies the answer to <topic> about the object behind <handle> into <buffer>.
The topics are in the table; the structures are in `<croi/syscall.h>` and the headers it includes.
Most topics need the INSPECT right.

A topic that returns one record needs <buffer_size> to hold it, else the call fails with `CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`.
A topic that returns a list fills as many records as fit and never fails for size.
The task and VMO topics marked "counts" below write the number of records written through <actual> and the number there are through <avail>, when those pointers are not null.
For a one-record topic both are 1 (and <actual> is 0 on `CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`).
The other topics ignore both pointers; pass null.
Pass <topic> as given: a topic that has grown carries a version in bits 31:28, and the shorter, older versions are accepted too.

| Topic | On | Right | Answer |
| `CROI_INFO_HANDLE_BASIC` | any handle | none | `croi_info_handle_basic_t`: koid, the handle's rights, type, the peer's koid for a channel |
| `CROI_INFO_HANDLE_COUNT` | any handle | INSPECT | `croi_info_handle_count_t`: handles to the object, in every table and in messages in flight |
| `CROI_INFO_PROCESS` | process | INSPECT | return code, start time, flags (started, exited, debugger attached); counts |
| `CROI_INFO_PROCESS_THREADS` | process | ENUMERATE | koids of its threads; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_PROCESS_MAPS` | process | INSPECT | the address space as `croi_info_maps_t`, depth first; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_PROCESS_VMOS` | process | INSPECT | `croi_info_vmo_t` for each VMO it holds or maps; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_TASK_STATS` | process | INSPECT | mapped, private, shared and scaled-shared bytes (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` once it is gone) |
| `CROI_INFO_HANDLE_TABLE` | process | INSPECT, MANAGE_PROCESS, MANAGE_THREAD | `croi_info_handle_extended_t` for each of its handles; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_THREAD` | thread | INSPECT | state, the kind of exception channel it waits on, affinity mask; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_THREAD_EXCEPTION_REPORT` | thread | INSPECT | the report of the exception it is in (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` if none); counts |
| `CROI_INFO_THREAD_STATS` | thread | INSPECT | run time and last CPU (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` once dead); counts |
| `CROI_INFO_THREAD_TRACE` | thread | INSPECT | the id trace records name it by |
| `CROI_INFO_TASK_RUNTIME` | thread, process, job | INSPECT | CPU time and queue time; a process's and a job's include their dead children; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_JOB` | job | INSPECT | return code, exited, debugger attached; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_JOB_CHILDREN` | job | ENUMERATE | koids of child jobs; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_JOB_PROCESSES` | job | ENUMERATE | koids of child processes; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_VMO` | VMO | none | `croi_info_vmo_t`; counts |
| `CROI_INFO_VMAR` | VMAR | INSPECT | `croi_info_vmar_t`: base and length |
| `CROI_INFO_VMAR_JIT` | VMAR | INSPECT | whether it is a reservation or a JIT one, and the mechanism |
| `CROI_INFO_BTI` | BTI | INSPECT | `croi_info_bti_t` |
| `CROI_INFO_BTI_PROPERTIES` | BTI | INSPECT | `croi_bti_properties_t` |
| `CROI_INFO_MSI` | MSI allocation | INSPECT | `croi_info_msi_t` |
| `CROI_INFO_RESOURCE` | resource | INSPECT | `croi_info_resource_t`: kind, flags, base, size, name |
| `CROI_INFO_MEMORY_ACCOUNT` | account | INSPECT | `croi_info_memory_account_t` |
| `CROI_INFO_DEBUGLOG` | log | none | `croi_info_debuglog_t`: records written, and printed to the console |
| `CROI_INFO_CPU_STATS` | INFO resource | see below | `croi_info_cpu_stats_t` for each CPU |
| `CROI_INFO_KMEM_STATS` | INFO resource | see below | `croi_info_kmem_stats_t`: the kernel's memory |
| `CROI_INFO_CPU_IDLE_STATES` | INFO resource | see below | `croi_idle_state_stats_t` for each CPU and state; counts |

The last three need <handle> to be a resource(2) that grants the INFO system resource (`CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_INFO_BASE`), or the root resource.
`CROI_INFO_CPU_STATS` returns how many records it wrote as the result of the call, as many as fit in <buffer_size>, and fails with `CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL` if none fits.
It fills the CPU number, the online flag, idle time, context switches, yields, interrupts, timer interrupts, timers run, syscalls and IPIs; the other fields are 0.
`CROI_INFO_KMEM_STATS` fills total, free, wired, heap, VMO, page table, other and the reclaim figures; the others are 0.
A topic the kernel does not know is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

`croi_vmo_size` reads a VMO's size through `CROI_INFO_VMO`.

## object_get_property, object_set_property

The properties are:

| Property | Value | On | Right |
| `CROI_PROP_NAME` | `char[32]` | process, thread, job, VMO, BTI; a resource's name is read only | GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY |
| `CROI_PROP_VMO_CONTENT_SIZE` | `uint64_t` | an anonymous or pager VMO | GET_PROPERTY; SET_PROPERTY and WRITE |
| `CROI_PROP_REGISTER_FS` | `uint64_t` | amd64: the calling thread's own handle | GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY |
| `CROI_PROP_REGISTER_GS` | `uint64_t` | amd64: the calling thread's own handle | GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY |
| `CROI_PROP_EXCEPTION_STATE` | `uint32_t` | an exception | GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY |
| `CROI_PROP_EXCEPTION_STRATEGY` | `uint32_t` | an exception | GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY |

A name is at most 31 bytes, cut at the first NUL.
To get it, <size> must be at least 32.
To set it, up to 31 bytes of <size> are read.
The name of a dead thread is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`, and an object of another type is `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE`.

The content size of a VMO is byte-granular and at most the VMO's size.
Setting it zeroes what lies between the smaller of the old and new size and the end of the VMO, and past the VMO's size is `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`.
Other kinds of VMO give `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

The FS and GS bases are the user thread-local-storage bases.
They can only be read or written on the calling thread's own handle (`CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED` for another thread, `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE` for a handle that is not a thread), and a base must be a user address.
On arm64 and rv64 the thread pointer register is user-writable, and these properties are `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

The exception properties are those of exception(2).

Except when a name is set, <size> must hold the value (`CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`).
Any other property is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

## object_get_child

`object_get_child` writes to <out> a handle to the child of <parent> whose koid is <koid>: a job's child job or child process, or a process's thread.
<parent> needs ENUMERATE.
<rights> is `CROI_RIGHT_SAME_RIGHTS`, for the parent handle's rights, or a subset of them (`CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED` otherwise).
A koid that is not a live child is `CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND`: a dead thread, process or job is not found.
A parent of another type is `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE`.

